In article <HAIRSTON.90Jul20122440@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>this is interesting (to me).  it needs to be developed some more,
>how do you propose identifying alignment (without access to r.g.h
>or source)?

You could have a message similar to what you get when you drink (un)holy
water as you attempt to wield the weapon. Or maybe something totally different,
for instance lawful character vs chaotic weapon: "You feel a great evil
emanating from this %s -more- It tries to reach into your spirit... -more-
Luckily, you manage to drop it to the floor in time" (or maybe you fail to do
so and change alignment and your god gets angry). Lawful vs lawful could be
"You feel a great holiness emanating from the %s" (resp "sense of peace" and
"gratifying evil" for the other alignments), and neutral vs non-neutral
might be "You sense a conflict between the %s and yourself... -more- The %s
evades your grasp and falls to the floor OR You manage to keep the weapon under
control". A scroll of identify should reveal the alignment of a named weapon,
and an altar should also do so but your god might get pissed off if you drop a
weapon of the "wrong" alignment on his altar (I don't think he should send a
demon or anything, but he should boom out something nasty and u.ugangr++, just
to make sure you don't do it all the time).

Come to think about it, artifacts with a strong ego should be more of a
liability rather than the mere tools they presently are. I don't see any
reason why Stormbringer would authorize you to set it aside and use Mjollnir
if it doesn't consider itself satiated, when it could so easily flip the
cursed flag :-) Generally speaking, evil weapons should be hard to get rid
of, "specialized" weapons should be ok as long as you're hitting "boring"
monsters but should jump into your hands (unless occupied with a cursed
object) when they find the kind of monster they want to hit and force you to
fight (no matter how eager you are to run away :-) ), and a lawful weapon
should do the same with "very evil" monsters such as demons, undeads, etc.

>how do you propose to keep balance (i.e. a neutral
>should have a defense against an elf wielding Stormbringer)? etc.

First of all, balance means equal opportunities for all alignments when it
comes to the number of available artifacts, otherwise a neutral character
would end up unable to use most of the stuff he is likely to encounter
(although, being neutral, he would be spared the high risks associated with
weapons of the "opposite alignment"). I'm not sure what you meant about the
Elf, but personally I would be inclined to rule that the game should not place
a named weapon into the hands of a monster who cannot use it properly - after
all, such a weapon would be likely to be coveted by other monsters who would
eventually manage to slay its owner, or get him to give it away since it
isn't much good for him. Or, even worse, the weapon might decide all by
itself that it likes the new prospective owner better and jump into its hands
right away ("The black blade decides that it likes the orc-captain better than
you, -more- and jumps into his hands! You are now empty-handed." *grin*).

>however, a wizard shouldn't be entirely loath to carrying a +11 Sunsword.

I don't see why he should: knowing his limitations, and the fact that wands
and spells have limited numbers of charges, I really don't see why he shouldn't
carry a good weapon if he finds one, plus who wants to waste a good spell for
a couple sewer rats? Actually, using his magic to improve this "backup"
solution seems perfectly appropriate to me. My point is that the game should
not force him into becoming a professional butcher because the dungeon has
got no magic; as soon as you reach, say, the 7th level, you will have a good
incentive to use magic if you're good at that and it takes you 20 attempts to
hit a giant ant or killer bee... A barbarian which can hit (and kill) one
after 2-3 attempts and keeps getting blinded when he reads spellbooks, not to
mention the fact that he invariably goofs up on the spell because of his
intelligence, is more likely to collect the spellbooks in order to sell them
to shopkeepers and buy good, useful armour with the money :-)

